= Introduction <sec:intro>

Dimensional regularisation with the Breitenlohner--Maison--'t Hooft--Veltman (BMHV) definition of $g5$ @tHooft:1972tcz @Breitenlohner:1977hr is the only treatment of chiral theories known to be consistent at every loop order. Its price is that the regularised action is not gauge invariant: the fermion kinetic term must be $d$-dimensional to regularise loops, while a chiral gauge coupling can only be four-dimensional. The symmetry is recovered order by order by finite, local counterterms that restore the Slavnov--Taylor (ST) identity of the renormalised effective action.

*What is delivered.* This report carries out that programme explicitly, diagram by diagram, for the Standard Model with one fermion family, gauge group $"SU"(3)_c times "SU"(2)_L times "U"(1)_Y$, in the Feynman gauge and with $overline("MS")$ subtraction. It follows the BMHV primer of A. E. Thomsen @Thomsen:starterpack (the "starter pack") and the scheme in which every chiral fermion has a sterile partner that is a singlet under all gauge groups @Belusca-Maito:2020ala @Fuentes-Martin:2025meh @Lazopoulos:2026two. The result is the complete one-loop renormalised action in the unbroken phase, $S_"ren" = S_0 + delta S^((1)) + Delta S^((1))_"ST"$: the tree-level action $S_0$, the singular counterterm $delta S^((1))$ (pure poles in $ep = (4 - d)\/2$, evanescent operators included) and the finite ST-restoring counterterm $Delta S^((1))_"ST"$. The same action in the broken phase follows from the shift of the Higgs field by its vacuum expectation value in the linear gauge used throughout; this needs no further loop input.

*Method.* The tree-level action breaks the ST identity by a single local operator $Dbrk_1$ of ghost number $1$, which couples a ghost, a physical fermion and its sterile partner. The one-loop 1PI functions of ghost number $0$ with external fields and BRST sources are computed with the $Rop$-operation, the expansion of the integrand around an auxiliary mass and the BMHV Dirac algebra; their poles give $delta S^((1))$. The one-loop 1PI functions with one insertion of $Dbrk_1$ give the finite breaking $X_1$: a local term, produced where an evanescent loop trace of order $ep$ multiplies an ultraviolet pole. The restoring counterterm solves the linear equation $bop Delta S^((1))_"ST" = -X_1$, with $bop$ the linearised ST operator. Every step is stated as an operation on Feynman graphs and Feynman rules, as the one-loop case of a construction defined at every order, and without prescriptions beyond the BMHV algebra and the ST identity.

*Main results.*
- The invariant part of $delta S^((1))$ and the renormalisation constants: @eq:c-dS-inv, @tab:c-Z; the one-loop beta functions: @eq:c-beta.
- The evanescent part of $delta S^((1))$: @eq:c-dS-ev; its $bop_d$ variation, which fixes the poles of the ghost-number-1 functions: @eq:c-bd-dS.
- The finite breaking $X_1$ class by class: @tab:sts-X; its anomaly part, which vanishes for the Standard-Model charges: @eq:sts-anom.
- The restoring counterterm $Delta S^((1))_"ST"$: @eq:c-DS; every operator with its divergent and finite coefficient: @tab:c-summary.
- The Feynman rules of all counterterm vertices, singular, evanescent and finite: @sec:ct-rules.
- The renormalised action in the broken phase: @sec:Sren-broken.

*Verification.* Each class was computed twice, independently. The poles of all ghost-number-1 classes agree with those predicted by $bop_d delta S^((1))$, evanescent parts included (@tab:sts-poles). The ST conditions were solved twice, by hand and as a numerical linear system at several coupling points reconstructed to exact rationals, with the same coefficients; $bop X_1 = 0$ holds, and $bop Delta S^((1))_"ST" + X_1 = 0$ was checked numerically. The restored identity was also checked with complete finite parts, independently of the evanescent tag: on the gauge two-point functions, on the Ward identities with Higgs legs and with fermion legs, and on the massless gauge bosons of the broken phase (@sec:st-checks). The supporting calculations are listed in @sec:supporting. $Delta S^((1))_"ST"$ agrees with @Belusca-Maito:2020ala (arXiv:2004.14398) and @Lazopoulos:2026two (arXiv:2610.38618) up to $bop$-closed terms (@sec:st-lit), and the beta functions agree with @Machacek:1983tz.

*Departures from the starter pack.* The overall sign of $Dbrk_1$ (@eq:Delta1) is opposite to eq. (1.18) of @Thomsen:starterpack read with the ghost as the leftmost factor; it agrees with @Belusca-Maito:2020ala and @Lazopoulos:2026two. The starter pack uses the symbol $Dbrk_1$ for two different operators: for $ST(S_("ren", l-1) + delta S^((l)))$ at $l = 1$ (its eq. (2.15)), which equals $ST(S_0 + delta S^((1)))$ and contains $bop_d delta S^((1))$, and for the tree-level breaking $ST(S_0)$ (its eq. (2.17)). This report keeps $Dbrk_1 = ST(S_0)$ for the tree-level breaking and writes the order-$l$ operator as $Dbrk^([l])$, with $Dbrk^([1]) = Dbrk_1 + bop_d delta S^((1))$ (@eq:m-Delta-l).

*Scope.* One fermion family, so the Yukawa couplings $y_u$, $y_d$, $y_e$ are real numbers without flavour mixing; Feynman gauge $xi = 1$. All loop calculations that determine $delta S^((1))$, $X_1$ and $Delta S^((1))_"ST"$ are done in the unbroken phase. In the broken phase one loop calculation is done as a check: the fermion-loop two-point functions of the photon, of the $Z$--photon mixing and of the gluon at zero momentum, which show that these gauge bosons stay massless (@sec:bl-massless). Not computed: two-loop quantities, the dependence on $xi$, all other broken-phase loop functions (in particular the two-point functions at non-zero momentum and the finite part of the one-loop tadpole) and the complex-mass scheme (@sec:bp-needed lists what they would require).

*Outline.* @sec:method states the method and translates each operator identity into an operation on diagrams. @sec:setup gives the conventions for symbols (@tab:alphabet), the regularised action, the BMHV algebra, the Feynman rules and the operator bases; a reader may consult its first sections for the notation while reading @sec:method. @sec:classes goes through every class of Green's functions and ends with the solution of the ST conditions (@sec:st-solution). @sec:conclusion states the renormalised action in both phases, with the Feynman rules of the counterterms. @sec:automation lists what the existing tools lack to automate the recipe. @sec:supporting lists the supporting material.
